SELECT     --- Retrieves data from a table
FROM	     --- Specifies the table
WHERE	     --- Filters rows based on a condition	
DISTINCT	 --- Removes duplicate values	
ORDER BY	 --- Sorts the results
GROUP BY	 --- Groups rows with the same values	
HAVING	   --- Filters grouped results	
LIMIT	     --- Restricts the number of returned rows 
AS	       --- Assigns an alias to a column or table

2. Data Manipulation Keywords (DML)
INSERT INTO	 --- Adds new records
UPDATE	     --- Modifies existing records
DELETE	     --- Removes records
REPLACE INTO ---	Inserts or replaces a record in MySQL

3. Data Definition Keywords (DDL)
CREATE	  --- Creates a database or table
ALTER	    --- Modifies a table structure
DROP	    --- Deletes a database object
TRUNCATE	--- Removes all rows from a table
RENAME	  ---Renames a database object (syntax varies)

4. Filtering and Condition Keywords
AND	      --- All conditions must be true
OR	      --- At least one condition must be true
NOT	      --- Reverses a condition
IN	      --- Matches any value in a list
BETWEEN	  --- Checks a range of values
LIKE	    --- Searches using a pattern
IS NULL	  --- Checks for missing or NULL values
EXISTS	  --- Checks whether a subquery returns rows
ANY	      --- Compares against any value returned by a subquery
ALL	      --- Compares against every value returned by a subquery

5. Aggregate Function Names
COUNT()	---Counts rows or non-NULL values
SUM()	  ---Calculates the total
AVG()	  ---Calculates the average
MIN()	  ---Finds the minimum value
MAX()	  ---Finds the maximum value

6. Joins and Relationships
JOIN	      --- Combines rows from tables
INNER JOIN  ---	Returns matching rows from both tables
LEFT JOIN	  --- Returns all left-table rows and matching right-table rows
RIGHT JOIN	--- Returns all right-table rows and matching left-table rows
CROSS JOIN	--- Returns combinations of rows from both tables
ON	        --- Specifies the join condition
UNION	      --- Combines results and removes duplicates
UNION ALL	  --- Combines results and keeps duplicates

7. Constraints and Keys
PRIMARY KEY  --- Uniquely identifies each row
FOREIGN KEY	 --- Establishes a relationship with another table
UNIQUE	     --- Prevents duplicate values in a constrained column or column combination
NOT NULL	   --- Prevents NULL values
DEFAULT	     --- Specifies a default value
CHECK	       --- Enforces a condition on values
REFERENCES	 --- Identifies the referenced table and columns
